    {
      "type": "Feature",
      "geometry": {
        "type": "Point",
        "coordinates": [
          77.4914619,
          12.929021,
          0
        ]
      },
      "properties": {
        "name": "Volagerehalli",
        "description": "<img src=\"https://mymaps.usercontent.google.com/hostedimage/m/*/3ADbDBKvaTXrOhGnQCKNblI5Z81kL38V5EVhPt5tepugRBr7J0pjJDwQbExHoAFxa1_9Z87Vlfxl182xU_j3iAmD_L4fOHvYeZBoYZhhcABbJg4jWQDm0CQUiw2n-f4NYGqFjz73yZRr1JeowNZS3VfBE12epGi6yYWrJ1Bx9_MvAVb27JATiNXLFHXQTMOSjz5g-jqfniQ0?authuser=0&fife=s16383\" height=\"200\" width=\"auto\" /><br><br>Epigraphia Carnatica Vol 9, Bangalore Taluq Inscription Number: 108<br>Hobli: Kengeri Hobli<br>Description of Installed Location in Historical Record: At Volagerehalli attached to Kengeri, on a stone in Khadar Sdb's field to the east.<br>Installation Date: 01/01/1060<br>Inscription Language: Grantha & Tamil<br>Size: 5'x4'<br>Current Status: Not traceable<br>English Translation Of The Inscription: 108<br>Date about 1060 A.D<br>(Tamil)- (This inscription is incomplete and contains only a portion of the historical introduction, which gave neither the name of the king nor the regnal year.)<br>Be it well. [In the …………year of the reign of Kopparakesarivarmar, alias sri-Rajendra-Devar, who, - ] having made the two noble ladies- the goddess of fortune and the goddess of the earth- his great queens; raised….his brilliant white umbrella; destroyed the strong kali; and bestowed beautiful crowns, set with jewels vying with one another in brilliancy, on his father’s younger brother, Gangaikonda-Solan of overpowering strength, with the title the proud Irumadi- Solan, the lord of Koli, who wore the warrior’s ankle-rings, with the title karikala-Solan, the lord of Koli, who wore the warrior’s ankle-rings, with the title Karikala-Sola, the lord of Koli, who wore the kan, whose strong and broad hand wielded the sword in warfare, with the title Sola-Gangan; on the good paranta-devan, endowed with strength of shoulders, with the title Sola- (A)yyoddhyarajan; among his sons, whom his sons, whom he affectionately cherished in his generous heart, on Irajendra-Solan praised on this earth, with the title Uttma-Solan, on Mudigonda-Soalan, who wore a garland made of a cluster of flowers and of fine opening buds, with the title Sola-Janakarajan of great valour, in whom the glory of the race of the Sun rests; on Irattapadikonda-Solan, who maintained the dignity of pure Tamil, with the title Sola-Vallaban ; among the sons of his sons, on Madurantakan, who wore sounding ankle-rings, brilliant as the sun, with title Sundara-Solan; and on the peerless Anaichchevagan, whose hand held a strong bow, with the title Nirupendra-Solan ;- was resplendent on earth ; and who –when the impetuous and angry Salukki Ahavamalla, - having heard the report that the Valavan (the Chola king), desirous of war, had marched upon Iratta-mandalam of superior strength, and had destroyed its fame………. And many districts and towns, - exclaimed, “ This is a disgrace to me!” sprang up, his eyes burning with rage, went to the beautiful great tirtha koppam, and commenced hostilities, though the shower of Ahavamalla’ straight arrows pierced his elephant’s forehead and his own thighs and mound like shoulders, and though the warriors wearing ankle-rings, who had mounted the warlike elephant along with him, fell; - not minding all this, directed (against the enemy) many matchless warlike weapons and sent to heaven Jayasingan, the younger brother of the valorous Chalukki, the warlike Pulakesi, the Igarland wearing Dasapanman; among proud princes, the mandalika (A) sokan, Araiyan of great renown……….. Mottayan, who wore a garland of opening buds full of honey, the unfailing Nanni-Nulamban, and other princes without number; whereupon the terrified Salukki, having been routed, along with the steady Revan, Tuttan, armed with a spear, Gundamayyam, whose hand held a death dealing weapon, and many other furious princes, fled, with disheveled hair, looking behind, with a bent back…………………………………<br><br>English Transliteration Of The Inscription: <br>Kannada Transliteration Of The Inscription: 108<br><br>ಕೆಂಗೇರಿಗೆ ಶೇರಿದ ವೊಳಗೆರೆಹಳ್ಳಿಗೆ ಪೂರ್ವದಿಕ್ಕಿನಲ್ಲಿ ಖಾದರಸಾಬಿಯ ಹೊಲದಲ್ಲಿ ಬಿದ್ದಿರುವದು<br><br>ಪ್ರಮಾಣ 5’ x 4’ – ಗ್ರಂಥ ಮತ್ತು ಅರವಕ್ಷರ<br>Tamil Transliteration Of The Inscription: Please help improve this map by providing us this. Please retype in Tamil the text of the inscription shown in the accompanying photos and email it to InscriptionStonesofBangalore@gmail.com. Thanks !<br>Telugu Transliteration Of The Inscription: NA<br>English Translation  Retyped By: Udaya Kumar P L <udayakumar.pl@gmail.com><br>English Transliteration  Retyped By: <br>Kannada Transliteration Retyped By: Udaya Kumar P L <udayakumar.pl@gmail.com><br>Photographs Provided By: Udaya Kumar P L <udayakumar.pl@gmail.com><br>Latitude: 12.929021<br>Longitude: 77.4914619<br>Name*: <br>EC 9 Ht#: <br>Description of Installed Location in Historical Records: <br>Hobli (as recorded in EC): ",
